      "math_academy_id": "q-128174",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222856/assets/0f18036ac5790bda530116c6847243108f9617f9ae12fcbc5c1b2c3fff018985.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Parallelogram\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Rhombus",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222856/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent sides, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where the sides are not all congruent. So it is **not** a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram), and II (rectangle).",
